// InspectResult is a **read-only projection of the declarations** of a package to be installed.
//
// # Why the kernel must provide it
//
// The only place that knows "what this package requests" is the manifest in the package, which is untrusted content
// before signature checking. Having the confirmation screen unpack it, parse the manifest and look up the permission definition table would put
// a parser of untrusted content into a process holding perm.permission.admin, the worst place in the whole system
// for a parser.
//
// nervud has to unpack and verify on the install path anyway, so reading out "the parsed declarations" comes for free.
//
// # It isn't a rehearsal of the install decision
//
// This method **doesn't answer "can this package be installed"**: signatures, digests, ABI, downgrade protection and the permission intersection
// are all decided on the Install side, and the state those decisions depend on (installed versions, signing lineage) may change between
// Inspect and Install. Giving out decision results in advance would make callers think
